# OpenDrive Bridge — credentials
#
# 1. Copy this file to .env, in this same folder:      cp .env.example .env
# 2. Fill in the two values below.
# 3. Start the daemon once:                            ./opendrived
#
# On that first start the bridge creates .env.key, generates its own API key,
# and rewrites this file encrypted. Your password stops being readable at that
# point, and you never have to touch either file again.
#
# What that protects you from, and what it does not (whitepaper §9.2.3):
#
#   It does protect you from committing your password to git, from syncing it to
#   a cloud backup in the clear, from someone reading it over your shoulder, and
#   from it turning up in a log or a process listing.
#
#   It does not protect you from someone who can already read your files as you,
#   because .env.key sits next to .env and they would get both. That is the
#   price of a daemon that can restart on its own without anybody typing a
#   passphrase. On a shared machine, use full-disk encryption and a separate
#   account rather than relying on this.
#
# Back up .env and .env.key together, or neither is any use.

ODB_USERNAME=you@example.com
ODB_PASSWORD=your-opendrive-password

# Everything below is optional and managed by the bridge itself. There is no
# need to set any of it by hand.
#
# ODB_API_KEY=          generated on first run; what odctl and other clients use
# ODB_ACCESS_TOKEN=     OAuth tokens, refreshed automatically
# ODB_REFRESH_TOKEN=
# ODB_SESSION_ID=
